Decode-sized MoE routing on a warp an expert (cuda/moe.zig routeWarp, kernels/cuda/moe.cu tf_moe_router_warp).

The router's logits (x . w for 256 experts + the shared gate, 3072 inputs) ran on moe.cu's tile kernel: 16 rows by
16 experts a block, each thread one fp32 FMA chain over all 3072 inputs from shared memory. For a decode step or a
verify window that is 17 blocks on 48 SMs and a 3072-long dependent chain a thread: ~63 us a layer, ~3.0 ms of a
38.5 ms one-row forward on a GB10 (measured by running the kernel twice in the captured graph). The new kernel gives
each expert a warp (128-thread blocks): lane l sums inputs 8l..8l+7 of every 256-input step in index order from
16-byte loads, then the warp adds its lanes in one fixed butterfly. A row's bits depend on that row alone at any row
count, so drafted replies still equal "draft": false.
qwen3_5_moe uses it for every non-prompt routing (decode, verify windows, prompt pieces of 16 rows or fewer, i.e.
where the decode experts run); prompt passes keep the tile kernel. TF_QWEN_ROUTER=tile restores the stock kernel.
Verified on a GB10 with Ornith-1.5-122B-NVFP4: one-row forward 38.8 -> 35.6 ms; tf-qwen35-cuda window (widths 1-64)
and tree logits equal step's bit for bit; teacher-forced NLL 1.2804 (tile) vs 1.2822 (warp), the same spread as
bf16 vs fp8 caches (routing flips from fp32 summation order), both under SGLang's 1.2848.

